  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 145 PM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Complex broad low pressure south of the area \nwill track northward tonight into Sunday allowing rain to spread\nacross the area. Exact placement of rainfall still uncertain \ngiven the complexities of the low pressure and where the main \nlow develops later tonight or Sunday.\n\nLatest surface analysis and obs shows a broad area of low \npressure across northern FL and eastern GA along a frontal \nboundary that extends eastward into the Atlantic. There may be a\nsecond low pressure area developing under convection over the \nAtlantic near 30N/74W as satellite imagery show some curved \nbands over the southern semi-circle of the potential low. \nMeanwhile high pressure is just off the NE coast. \n\nThe low over FL/GA is expected to continue to move northward \ntoday into tonight and eventually become weaker as the low or \nwave to the east becomes the dominate low as it approaches the \nOuter Banks. However, the timing and location of this still \nremains highly uncertain and this will unfortunately be the main\nplayer in how much rainfall occurs at any one location (or if \nrain even falls as some guidance such as the 12z GFS suggest \nvery little precipitation over the area tomorrow). If the \nwestern low dissipates quickly and the coastal low takes over, \ninland areas may not see as much rainfall as the inland rainfall\nwill be dependent on the forcing from the western low. \nMeanwhile, if the coastal low stays too far offshore, much of \nthe area (even at the coast) will see very little rainfall. \nOverall, the 1\" rainfall probabilities have decreased inland and\nas such have also lowered precipitation amounts inland. We are \nstill thinking that rain will spread northward and into the area\ntomorrow as the western low will likely maintain itself longer \nthan what the GFS suggests. In addition, the PoPs across the \nsoutheast will remain lower as it looks like the best forcing \nfrom any coastal low development will stay offshore and north of\nthat area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mild on Monday, then much cooler through mid \nweek. Increasing potential for coastal low Wednesday or Thursday\nwhich may prove impactful especially at the coast. \n\nMonday will be mild behind the coastal low and before the \nstronger high pressure builds over the area. Widespread \ntemperatures in the low-mid 70s expected. Much cooler Tuesday \nand Wednesday with below normal temps but dry. Then attention \nturns to the potential system for around Thanksgiving.\n\nEnsemble consensus is in fair agreement in low pressure \ndeveloping in the eastern Gulf of America or off the southeast \ncoast and moving parallel to the coast by Thanksgiving. There \nare still placement and timing disagreements which will \noriginate Winchell from the systems over the eastern Pacific \nthis weekend and early next week. However, there is an \nincreasing likelihood of at least some impacts at the coast \nsimply due to the tight gradient between the expected 1040+mb \nhigh over New England and low pressure to the south leading to \nprolonged and increasing NE winds. In addition, this has the \npotential to bring better rainfall to the area with a decent \ntropical connection and strong IVT nearby. Plus, NE winds \nespecially near the coast could be quite gusty due this pressure\ngradient and coastal flooding looks possible especially with \nthe King Tide. This is still 5 days out, and details will be \nbetter understood by early next week as the system over the \nPacific moves onshore over the west coast.",
